# Project: Stickman Run

A tiny browser jump & run game. Students ask for "annoying" features (spikes, holes,
moving platforms, fake doors, ...) and want to see the result quickly.

## Files
- `game.js` — the whole game (level data, physics, drawing, main loop). Almost every change goes here.
- `index.html`, `style.css` — page shell. Rarely needs changes.

## How game.js is organised
- `level` holds the level data. Everything is a rectangle `{ x, y, w, h }`, y grows downwards.
  Canvas is 960 × 540, ground top is at `GROUND_Y` (460).
- Holes: split `level.platforms` into separate platforms with a gap.
- New obstacle types: add an array to `level` (e.g. `spikes: [...]`), check it in `update()`
  (use `overlaps(player, thing)` and call `die()`), and draw it in `draw()` with a small `drawXxx()` function.
- Moving things: give them speed fields and update their position in `update(dt)` (always multiply by `dt`).

## Rules for changes (speed matters!)
- Keep everything in `game.js`. Plain JavaScript + canvas only. Do NOT add libraries or npm packages.
- Make the smallest change that does what was asked. Don't refactor or rewrite unrelated code.
- Draw graphics with simple canvas shapes; no image files.
- The dev server is already running with auto-reload. Do NOT run npm commands, start servers,
  or open the browser to test — the student will check the game themselves.
- After editing, reply with one or two sentences describing what changed and how to try it.
